---
// The reference soybean article - since 4 October 2026 it merges the two
// articles that partly repeated each other (see the French page for the
// details). The old address redirects here (astro.config.mjs).
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import figProcede from '../../../assets/img/fig-procede-soja.webp';
---

<ArticleLayout slug="soybean-processing-method">
  <p>
    Without prior heat treatment, the nutritional value and the protein
    digestibility of raw soybean are relatively low. The bean contains
    biologically active substances with an antinutritional effect:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>proteases (trypsin inhibitors), which prevent the beans from being digested,</li>
    <li>haemagglutinins (lectins), which prevent the absorption of nutrients,</li>
    <li>allergenic factors, which can affect milk-fed calves in particular,</li>
    <li>saponins, and factors inducing goitre and rickets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why extrude soybean</h2>
  <p>
    To make the most of the nutritional components of soybean, extrusion is
    the best treatment. Its aim is threefold:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>to reduce the content of antinutritional factors,</li>
    <li>not to damage the proteins,</li>
    <li>
      to rupture the cells containing the oil, in order to improve the energy
      value.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Compared with the raw bean, extrusion improves protein digestibility by
    11%, lysine by 14%, methionine by 11% and cystine by 5%. It also lowers
    the crude amino acid content, and therefore the undigested protein
    fraction.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens inside the extruder</h2>
  <p>
    The ground beans are compressed inside a barrel by a screw turning at
    high speed, then forced through a die. This is dry extrusion: the heat
    comes from the mechanical energy created by the friction and shear
    forces inside the machine. The beans are brought to around 150&nbsp;°C -
    160&nbsp;°C at the die outlet - for a very short time, a few tens of
    seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the barrel, the free water in the bean rises well above its
    vaporisation point, but remains liquid under a pressure of several tens
    of bars. At the die outlet, the abrupt return to atmospheric pressure
    causes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      instant evaporation of this water - around 6% of the initial weight of
      the beans -, bringing the extruded beans to a moisture level of 6%,
    </li>
    <li>
      the bursting of the cells containing the oil, which makes the work of
      the press easier afterwards,
    </li>
    <li>
      an abrupt drop in temperature, to around 100&nbsp;°C, which halts
      practically all browning reactions.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This method is therefore akin to HT-ST (High Temperature-Short Time)
    treatments, somewhat similar to the UHT treatment used to produce
    long-life milk.
  </p>

  <h2>A more energetic, more stable product</h2>
  <h3>The energy content is higher</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>because the water content of the product is low,</li>
    <li>
      because extrusion destroys practically all the cell structures of the
      bean, which allows better digestion of the energy it contains.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>The product keeps for months</h3>
  <p>
    Extrusion destroys the lipases and the lipoxidase of the raw bean - these
    enzymes promote the hydrolysis and the oxidation of fats. With its low
    free water content, the product, stored properly for months, does not
    turn rancid, does not oxidise and does not go mouldy, with no
    preservatives added.
  </p>

  <h3>Digestibility of soybean-based rations</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table-data">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Effect of the processing method on the digestibility of rations
        based on soybeans, in percent
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Digestibility (%)</th>
          <th scope="col">Toasting + fine grinding</th>
          <th scope="col">Toasting + coarse grinding</th>
          <th scope="col">Extrusion</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Oil (adjusted)</th><td>63.0</td><td>63.9</td><td>85.7</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Nitrogen</th><td>80.1</td><td>78.9</td><td>86.8</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Energy</th><td>82.0</td><td>81.8</td><td>89.7</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p><small>Source: Wiseman, 1984.</small></p>

  <h3>Metabolisable energy in broiler chickens</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table-data">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Influence of the processing method on the apparent metabolisable
        energy of whole soybeans in broiler chickens
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Process</th>
          <th scope="col">AME (MJ/kg of dry matter)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Dry extrusion</th><td>17.9</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Wet extrusion</th><td>17.4</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Micronisation</th><td>17.5</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Soybean meal + oil</th><td>17.5</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Toasting</th><td>15.8</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Jet-sploding</th><td>15.5</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Raw beans</th><td>13.5</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <small>AME = apparent metabolisable energy. Source: Adams and Jensen, 1985.</small>
  </p>

  <h2 id="oil-extraction">Extrusion then pressing: recovering the oil</h2>
  <p>
    To obtain a product with a reasonable energy contribution, making it
    easier to balance the ration, it is worth partially de-oiling the
    extruded beans. The fat content falls from 20% to around 5.4% in the
    meal, and a high value-added product is recovered: soybean oil. In
    animal feed, the protein level is what matters most: lowering the fat
    content raises the protein content of the meal, while recovering a
    co-product.
  </p>
  <p>
    The conventional process has two phases, as the diagram below shows. To
    lower the fat content of the meal further, one solution under study
    consists in adding a special chamber to the extruder barrel to recover
    oil. The fat content at the extruder outlet is then 1 to 2% lower, which
    makes the work of the press easier.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Image
      src={figProcede}
      alt="Diagram of the two-phase soybean processing method: extrusion then pressing"
      widths={[600, 900]}
      width={900}
      quality={70}
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 700px"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>The conventional process, in two phases.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Extrusion de-oils the beans better than a flaking-cooking-pressing
    method: at the extruder outlet, the cells containing the fats have been
    destroyed and the oil is free. The extruder works as a preparation stage
    for the press, which then has a better yield, a higher throughput and
    less wear. Trials show that the meal contains on average 1.3% less oil,
    with higher yields of both oil and meal, and less loss.
  </p>

  <h3>Mass balance</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table-data">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Mass balance per 1,000 kilograms of beans, extrusion-pressing versus
